WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.870
PQC migration starts with knowing where cryptography is actually used.

2
00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:30.735
That sounds simple until an organization tries to enumerate certificates, TLS endpoints, VPN profiles, SSH keys, API clients, code-signing systems, firmware-signing processes, package repositories, database encryption, backups, embedded devices, cloud services, third-party products, cryptographic libraries, hardware security modules, and key management platforms.

3
00:00:30.735 --> 00:00:36.723
Cryptography often hides inside products, dependencies, and default configurations.

4
00:00:36.723 --> 00:00:40.475
A useful inventory records more than an algorithm name.

5
00:00:40.475 --> 00:01:01.550
It should capture the business owner, technical owner, system purpose, cryptographic role, library or product dependency, key location, certificate authority, data sensitivity, data retention requirement, external exposure, operational criticality, vendor involvement, and current migration status.

6
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:04.823
Without that context, teams cannot prioritize.

7
00:01:04.823 --> 00:01:14.084
A test system with no sensitive data does not carry the same urgency as an internet-facing service protecting long-lived regulated data.

8
00:01:14.084 --> 00:01:32.046
Discovery is difficult because the same organization may have centralized platforms, independently managed applications, old appliances, unmanaged certificates, developer-created integrations, cloud services, and legacy systems that no one wants to touch.

9
00:01:32.046 --> 00:01:35.239
Some cryptography is visible in network scans.

10
00:01:35.239 --> 00:01:38.193
Some appears in configuration management.

11
00:01:38.193 --> 00:01:44.340
Some lives in CI/CD pipelines, mobile apps, update channels, or vendor-managed services.

12
00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:50.088
Some requires interviews with teams that understand how a system is really operated.

13
00:01:50.088 --> 00:01:53.281
The goal is not a perfect spreadsheet on day one.

14
00:01:53.281 --> 00:01:59.588
The goal is a living cryptographic inventory that improves over time and supports decisions.

15
00:01:59.588 --> 00:02:10.764
Start with exposed services and long-lived confidential data, then expand into internal systems, software supply chain functions, and third-party dependencies.

16
00:02:10.764 --> 00:02:22.659
Good inventory work turns PQC from an abstract concern into a concrete roadmap: here are the systems, here are the roles, here are the blockers, and here is what can be tested next.
